WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.102
This chapter recalls the foundations needed for the
case study: significant decisions, intended

00:00:06.102 --> 00:00:09.184
versus implemented structure and multiple views.

00:00:09.184 --> 00:00:15.222
The extended deck repeats much of the original
introduction, so use those sections for review.

00:00:15.222 --> 00:00:20.810
Then apply the vocabulary to one concrete promise
about durable submission and reliable

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:21.645
confirmation.

00:00:21.645 --> 00:00:27.650
Generated code can accelerate visible progress while
leaving questions about understanding and

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:28.992
rationale unresolved.

00:00:28.992 --> 00:00:32.122
Distinguish technical, cognitive and intent debt.

00:00:32.122 --> 00:00:37.361
Architecture helps teams define promises, explain
structure and evaluate evidence.

00:00:37.361 --> 00:00:43.750
Review generated implementations through concrete
scenarios and failure tests rather than treating a

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.008
working demonstration as complete proof of quality.

00:00:47.045 --> 00:00:51.286
Start with the student’s uncertainty: did the
submission succeed?

00:00:51.286 --> 00:00:55.332
Identify stakeholder needs and a trustworthy source
of status.

00:00:55.332 --> 00:00:59.573
The LEARN-inspired architecture is a hypothetical
teaching model.

00:00:59.573 --> 00:01:05.120
Its responsibility boxes explain cooperation without
committing to separate services.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:11.515
Include interruptions, concurrency and authorization
in the problem before choosing implementation

00:01:11.515 --> 00:01:12.037
details.
